.temp/TASK-260830-21gygk-mutants-rev14-02/mutation-source/LOGBOOK.md:725:- ROOT CAUSE: entry 1632's generalisation, for the third recorded time. Round 2 closed the four guards the review named and did not enumerate the rest of the path. `OwnerPolicy.Approves` is the next guard inside `trustCandidate` after the two that were fixed, and `TestDiscoverRefusesRelativePluginDir` is the single-entry twin of the multi-entry PATH fixture the same round wrote — narrowing the `plugin_dirs` absolute gate to `index == 0` also survives the full suite. The remedy for "proven at one sample" is the traversal, not the named fix.
.temp/TASK-260830-21gygk-mutants-rev14-02/mutation-source/LOGBOOK.md:733:- SCOPE: `internal/provider` review of TASK-260830-2890sd at `292fc6d`; §7.1. Five mutants survived the full suite.
.temp/TASK-260830-21gygk-mutants-rev14-02/mutation-source/LOGBOOK.md:736:- FINDING: the trust gate is proven at 1 of 3 discovery sources. Injecting `if source == "path" { info.IsRegular = true; info.UID = owner.OperatorUID }` into `trustCandidate` survives, as does the same for every `source != "plugin_dirs[0]"`. All four refusal tests use a single `/plugins` directory; PATH is exercised only positively and for duplicates. Entry 1632's rule — enumerate every guard on the path and measure each over its own domain — applies per SOURCE here, not only per guard.
.temp/TASK-260830-21gygk-mutants-rev14-02/mutation-source/LOGBOOK.md:738:- FINDING: the digest guard is proven at 1 of 32 bytes; narrowing `provider.go:466` to `sum[:1]` survives, because both fixture pairs happen to differ in their first byte. The `identity != record.owner` half of `provider.go:458` has no test at all — `adminUID = 7` is declared at `fake_test.go:118` and never used, and it is exactly the fixture that case needs.
.temp/TASK-260830-21gygk-mutants-rev14-02/mutation-source/LOGBOOK.md:760:- FINDING: the traversal immediately produced three more instances of the class, none of which any reviewer had named. The `ErrForeignDocument` branch was covered by one schema identifier, so restricting it to that identifier and routing every other foreign schema into `readSuccess` survived the suite. The "schema member is not a string" guard was covered by the number `5`, so restricting it to JSON numbers survived. Both are the round-6 shape: not an admission bypass, a change in WHICH FACT the machine client is told. The presence guard for a missing `schema` member survives an arbitrary predicate keyed on the exit status, which is the class round 4 declined to block on and is recorded as a stated bound rather than fixed.
.temp/TASK-260830-21gygk-mutants-rev14-02/mutation-source/LOGBOOK.md:764:- FINDING (reviewer observation, correction to the record): three mutants were dismissed in round 5 on the grounds that "none is a registered code". `terminal_backend_capability_unproven` IS registered at Structured Error 1.3.0 exit 6 (`catalog_gen.go:541`), and sparing it in `ExitCodeFor`'s version scoping survives on a clean tree. The conclusion holds for a different reason — the same narrowing on `observation_gap` is killed by `TestExitCodeForRefusesVersionAndCodeDrift`, so the class has a covered representative. The dismissal stands; the sentence behind it did not. A survivor dismissed with a wrong reason is worse than one dismissed with none, because the reason is what the next round trusts.
.temp/TASK-260830-21gygk-mutants-rev14-02/mutation-source/LOGBOOK.md:767:- EVIDENCE: harness grown from 63 to 86 mutants — 85 killed, 1 declared subsumed (unchanged), 0 unexplained survivors, 64 narrowing by the harness's own definition, suite restored green after every batch. Supersedes the "63 mutants at 62 killed" figure in the 1447 entry below. One mutant on the §1.6 disclosure was DISCARDED as equivalent and replaced: disabling the assertion in the test changes no outcome while the gap is open, so the replacement closes the gap in production and requires the pin to redden. Same stated bound: the harness mutates the tree, lives under `.temp/`, and no committed artifact derives its counts.
.temp/TASK-260830-21gygk-mutants-rev14-02/mutation-source/LOGBOOK.md:773:- FINDING: this was a reachable bypass and not only an unmeasured ratio. `code` and `exit_code` are two independent members of one document, and this guard is the sole thing binding them. The retryability refusal this package publishes keys on the EXIT STATUS for three whole classes — 7 authorization, 16 policy refusal, 130 interrupt — so relabelling a code out of its own class asks that refusal about the wrong class and it stays silent. Under the narrowing, `{"code":"authentication_failed","exit_code":9,"retryable":true}` was ADMITTED by `cliresult.Read` at exit status 9, and the caller received a `Reading` naming an authorization failure with `Retryable() == true`. The §14.2 exit-status corroboration cannot see it, because the process really did exit with the relabelled status.
.temp/TASK-260830-21gygk-mutants-rev14-02/mutation-source/LOGBOOK.md:777:- FINDING (reviewer observation, closed in the same pass): the registered-status admission in `decodeExitStatus` was driven at `{0, 1, 18, 99}`, so `!IsFailureExitStatus(status) && status != 42` survived the whole suite. The complement of a four-value sample is not a bound. `TestTheExitStatusAdmissionIsSweptOverEveryByteValue` sweeps 0..255 plus six out-of-range values and requires admission if and only if the §15.2 table registers the status as a failure.
.temp/TASK-260830-21gygk-mutants-rev14-02/mutation-source/LOGBOOK.md:778:- DECISION: an oracle must not be the predicate under test. The sweep above enumerates the expected admissions through `ExitStatusMeaning`, not `IsFailureExitStatus`, because the latter IS the decision being measured and an oracle built from it would agree with whatever the mutant narrowed it to. The asserted row count of seventeen is what catches a mutation that moves both. The sweep also drives a code the registry does not carry, which takes the §15.3 unknown-code branch and skips the agreement guard, so exactly one decision is left in the row.
.temp/TASK-260830-21gygk-mutants-rev14-02/mutation-source/LOGBOOK.md:781:- EVIDENCE: harness grown from 53 to 63 mutants — 62 killed, 1 declared subsumed (unchanged), 0 unexplained survivors, 44 narrowing by the harness's own definition, suite restored green. Supersedes the "53 mutants at 52 killed" figure in the 1421 entry below. Same stated bound: the harness mutates the tree, lives under `.temp/`, and no committed artifact derives its counts.
.temp/TASK-260830-21gygk-mutants-rev14-02/mutation-source/LOGBOOK.md:791:- FINDING (reviewer Observation A, extended and acted on rather than recorded as a bound): the reviewer measured that narrowing `axerror.requireCommonDataModel` to skip documents over 4096 bytes SURVIVES, because every duplicate-member fixture is hand-built and short, and declined to block because byte length is not a §1.6 dimension. The identical narrowing on `documentSchema`'s copy of the same gate in `cliresult` also survived — the finding was reported for one reader and holds in both. Both are closed with a conforming document several times that size: a Structured Error padded through its 64-key `details` bound, and a CLI Result carrying twelve Session Summaries.
.temp/TASK-260830-21gygk-mutants-rev14-02/mutation-source/LOGBOOK.md:792:- DECISION: give a size fixture ONE knob. The first padding helper padded through both the 4096-character `message` bound and the `details` map, so narrowing either loop alone left the document over 4096 and the mutant survived as an equivalent one. Padding through `details` alone makes the narrowed loop redden the row's own length assertion. A fixture whose size has two independent sources cannot be shown to fail closed.
.temp/TASK-260830-21gygk-mutants-rev14-02/mutation-source/LOGBOOK.md:795:- EVIDENCE: harness grown from 44 to 53 mutants — 52 killed, 1 declared subsumed (unchanged), 0 unexplained survivors, 36 narrowing by the harness's own definition, suite restored green. Supersedes the "44 mutants at 43 killed" figure in the 1338 entry below. Same stated bound as that entry: the harness mutates the tree, lives under `.temp/`, and no committed artifact derives its counts.
.temp/TASK-260830-21gygk-mutants-rev14-02/mutation-source/LOGBOOK.md:806:- FINDING (reviewer Observation A, acted on): `exitStatusIsNotEnough` documents its own count as "a measured count rather than as advice" and `README.md` repeated the claim, but every assertion on those refusals matched the trailing clause `identifies no failure` and nothing read the number. Replacing `len(fanIn[exitStatus])` with a constant, or the whole `%d registered` clause with the word "many", left the suite green. Both are killed now, and so is the narrowing that keeps the count correct at exit 6 only — which is exactly the single assertion proposed as the minimal fix. The sweep drives every registered command at every registered §15.2 failure status through `Read` and re-derives each answer from `CodesByExitStatus`.
.temp/TASK-260830-21gygk-mutants-rev14-02/mutation-source/LOGBOOK.md:807:- FINDING (reviewer Observation B, acted on): retry independence from the exit status was proven at exit 12 alone, and the one exit-15 envelope any test drove already declared `retryable: true`, so a fabricated "if the status is 15, retry" branch returned the answer the document would have given and survived. `TestTheRetryDecisionFollowsTheDocumentAtEveryFailureStatus` drives both polarities across the whole failure domain; the true arm covers fourteen of seventeen statuses because the pinned document forbids the claim for every code of the other three, and that figure is pinned rather than left implicit.
.temp/TASK-260830-21gygk-mutants-rev14-02/mutation-source/LOGBOOK.md:808:- DECISION (reviewer Observation C, left alone): the two survivors the round-3 reviewer checked as genuinely equivalent — `Succeeded()` recomputed from `exitStatus`, and `ExitStatus()` returned from `failure.ExitCode()` — are NOT "fixed". They survive because `readSuccess` and `readFailure` between them make document and status provably agree on every admitted reading. A survivor dismissed with a reason is evidence; one quietly fixed hides why it survived. The same holds for the two round-2 dismissals, the near-equivalent `details` narrowing of `requireCommonDataModel` and the `CodesByExitStatus` empty-group pre-seed.
.temp/TASK-260830-21gygk-mutants-rev14-02/mutation-source/LOGBOOK.md:810:- EVIDENCE: harness grown from 28 to 44 mutants — 43 killed, 1 declared subsumed (unchanged), 0 unexplained survivors, suite restored green. Supersedes the "28 mutants at 27 killed" figure in the 1231 entry below.
.temp/TASK-260830-21gygk-mutants-rev14-02/mutation-source/LOGBOOK.md:814:### 1231 — A gate proven at one sample is a gate proven at one sample: both §14.2 outcome guards shipped with a narrowing the suite could not see
.temp/TASK-260830-21gygk-mutants-rev14-02/mutation-source/LOGBOOK.md:816:- SCOPE: `internal/cliresult/client_test.go` (two new sweeps, one new test), README mutation prose, `internal/traceability` ownership registration. Round-3 rework of TASK-260830-33sfxc after review; §14.2, §15.2, §15.3. No production code changed — the implementation was correct in both findings and was not asked to move.
.temp/TASK-260830-21gygk-mutants-rev14-02/mutation-source/LOGBOOK.md:817:- ROOT CAUSE: this leaf published the standard "a gate whose only evidence is that deleting it reddens something has been shown to exist, not to hold" and then failed it on its own two `ErrOutcomeDisagreement` guards. Each was driven at exactly one row, so each admitted a narrowing that the whole suite could not distinguish from the real guard.
.temp/TASK-260830-21gygk-mutants-rev14-02/mutation-source/LOGBOOK.md:818:- FINDING (F1): `if failure.CodeRegistered() && failure.ExitCode() != output.ExitStatus` survived `go test ./internal/cliresult ./internal/axerror`. The uncovered row is precisely where the guard is the SOLE enforcement: `axerror.decodeBody` cross-checks `exit_code` against the pinned registry only when `ExitCodeFor` resolves, and for a code a later compatible minor added it takes the `ErrUnregisteredCode` branch and runs no exit check at all. For a registered code the equality is bound twice; for an unregistered one, `readFailure` is all there is. Reproduced with the frozen `error-1.0.0-later-minor-code.json`: a machine client is handed exit class 5, "workspace/native-store conflict; no silent overwrite", for a document declaring exit class 10, ownership/lease/fencing — two different remediations, chosen from the wrong one.
.temp/TASK-260830-21gygk-mutants-rev14-02/mutation-source/LOGBOOK.md:820:- FINDING (F2): `if output.ExitStatus != SuccessExitStatus && output.ExitStatus != 130` survived the same suites, admitting a CLI Result success object at exit 130 as `Succeeded() == true`. Exit 130 is the §15.2 row "interrupted by operator signal before a clean response", which makes it the one failure status at which a success document plausibly does reach stdout before the process dies — so a narrowing at exactly that status is a realistic bypass, not an artificial one.
.temp/TASK-260830-21gygk-mutants-rev14-02/mutation-source/LOGBOOK.md:823:- EVIDENCE: harness grown from 23 to 28 mutants — 27 killed, 1 declared subsumed (unchanged), 0 unexplained survivors, each mutant verified applied, compiled, and carrying the MUTATED text on disk before measurement, suite restored green. Fourteen now narrow rather than delete. Both round-2 survivors were reproduced as SURVIVED on the unchanged tree first and are KILLED after; supersedes the "23 mutants at 22 killed" figure in the 1157 entry below.
.temp/TASK-260830-21gygk-mutants-rev14-02/mutation-source/LOGBOOK.md:826:- NOTE: the two survivors the reviewer checked and dismissed were left alone as instructed — the `details`-bearing narrowing of `requireCommonDataModel` is near-equivalent on conforming input, and pre-seeding an empty group per failure status in `CodesByExitStatus` is genuinely equivalent against today's registry. A survivor dismissed with a reason is evidence; a survivor quietly fixed hides why it survived.
.temp/TASK-260830-21gygk-mutants-rev14-02/mutation-source/LOGBOOK.md:842:- FINDING (F3): `TestReadDistinguishesAbsenceFromAReadFailure` asserted `errors.Is` membership only, so aliasing `ErrAbsentDocument` onto `ErrUnreadableDocument` survived the suite. The first mutual-exclusion fix **also survived**: it skipped "the sentinel this row wanted" by comparing VALUES, and under the alias the two values are equal, so it skipped exactly the comparison that would have caught it. The rows now name their sentinel as a string. A negative assertion written in terms of the thing it is testing for is not a negative assertion.
.temp/TASK-260830-21gygk-mutants-rev14-02/mutation-source/LOGBOOK.md:843:- FINDING: the first draft of this fix cited Section 1.6 as fixing the wire format as "UTF-8 JSON restricted to the common logical data model". **That sentence is nowhere in the pinned document** — a paraphrase wearing quotation marks, caught by checking the citation through `internal/specdoc` instead of trusting the wording. The two real fragments, `SPEC.md:218` "map keys MUST be UTF-8 strings and MUST be unique" and `SPEC.md:221` "floating-point numbers, NaN, Infinity, non-string map keys, and duplicate keys are forbidden", are now located BY CONTENT and their section is re-derived, so the citation survives re-pagination; the absence of the paraphrase is asserted too, so the correction cannot be undone by someone who remembers the wording. Same class as the 0559 false-citation entry, one contract over.
.temp/TASK-260830-21gygk-mutants-rev14-02/mutation-source/LOGBOOK.md:844:- EVIDENCE: 23 mutants, each verified applied, compiled, and carrying the MUTATED text on disk before measurement; 22 killed, 1 declared subsumed, 0 unexplained survivors. Nine narrow rather than delete. `go build`, `go vet`, `gofmt -l` clean; `go test ./... -count=1` exit 0 across 13 packages; `-cover` exit 0 with `cliresult` 95.5% and `axerror` 99.5%; `tracecheck` exit 0 at `acceptance_cases` 73 → 74 and `clauses_discharged` 17/403 unchanged — the new acceptance case is attached to the §15.1 and §17.2 bindings as evidence and claims no additional clause, because the gate discharges no obligation the clause scanner measures.
.temp/TASK-260830-21gygk-mutants-rev14-02/mutation-source/LOGBOOK.md:858:- FINDING: one mutant is **declared subsumed**: removing the exit-0 guard in `readFailure` leaves the suite green because `decodeExitStatus` never admits a Structured Error carrying `exit_code` 0, so the equality check refuses the same input one guard later. 14 mutants, each verified applied and compiled before measurement, 13 killed, 1 subsumed, 0 unexplained survivors. Superseded by the 1157 entry above: the harness is now 23 mutants at 22 killed / 1 subsumed, and the two additional survivors that appeared when the data-model gate landed were closed rather than declared.
.temp/TASK-260830-21gygk-mutants-rev14-02/mutation-source/LOGBOOK.md:874:- FINDING: two mutants survive and are **declared subsumed** rather than tolerated. The SessionSummary `capabilities` count bound `[0..7]` is unreachable because the capability vocabulary has exactly seven members, so a map of admitted names can never exceed it; and `decodeRequiredUUIDv7OrNull`'s presence guard is unreachable because `decodeClosedDocument` already refuses a document missing any of the eight declared members. Both keep an assertion pinning the invariant that makes them subsumed, so the day the vocabulary grows the subsumption claim reddens.
.temp/TASK-260830-21gygk-mutants-rev14-02/mutation-source/LOGBOOK.md:878:- FIX (residual R1 from leaf 1's review): `minRedactableCause = 8` decides which causes the causal-leak gate scans at all, and every cause in the corpus was at least 64 characters, so raising it to 16 or 64 left the suite green - the bound was proved by deleting the gate rather than by narrowing it. `TestMinimumRedactableCauseIsPinnedAtItsBoundary` now uses a cause of exactly 8 characters that must be scanned and one of 7 that must be skipped, on the message and on a diagnostic value, plus a multi-byte fixture that fails a byte-counted bound. Verified: 7, 9 and 16 each redden.
.temp/TASK-260830-21gygk-mutants-rev14-02/mutation-source/LOGBOOK.md:886:- FINDING: every §15.1 detail bound was defeated by this, and **no test covered post-construction mutation** - all 20 mutants and the whole positive/negative suite only ever validated maps nobody touched afterwards. The exported doc comment claimed "the closed Section 15.1 object is the only shape this type can produce", which this board treats as a contract; it was false and undisclosed. `internal/config` already returns an isolated resolved-root snapshot for exactly this reason, so the convention existed and was not followed.
.temp/TASK-260830-21gygk-mutants-rev14-02/mutation-source/LOGBOOK.md:887:- FIX: deep copy on the way in (`cloneDetails` → `cloneDetailValue`) and on the way out (`Detail`). Five new mutants, three of them **narrowings** rather than deletions - copy shallow again, deep copy stopped at one level, deep copy applied to maps but not arrays - and the tests attack containers at three depths and on both sides of an array so a one-level copy fails them. `internal/axerror/ownership_test.go`.
.temp/TASK-260830-21gygk-mutants-rev14-02/mutation-source/LOGBOOK.md:891:- NOTE: the 0742 entry credits a leading-quote guard in `decodeExitStatus` as the fix for the `"9"` string-exit-status defect. It was not - the real fix was reading `exit_code` from `json.RawMessage`, and the guard was unkillable dead weight in front of `strconv.ParseInt`, which already refuses quoted bytes. Guard removed, the refusal table widened to `"9"`, `""`, `9.5`, `1e1`, `true`, `[]`, `{}`, `[9]`, and a mutant that strips quotes before parsing - the exact narrowing a `json.Number` field would reintroduce - now reddens.
.temp/TASK-260830-21gygk-mutants-rev14-02/mutation-source/LOGBOOK.md:892:- NOTE: rev1's harness verified several deletion mutants by grepping for an **unrelated** string that was present either way, which cannot distinguish "mutation applied" from "perl no-op". `run_mutant` now takes `present|absent` and a deletion is verified by the absence of the exact text it deleted.
.temp/TASK-260830-21gygk-mutants-rev14-02/mutation-source/LOGBOOK.md:893:- EVIDENCE: 26 mutants, each verified applied before measurement, 26 killed, 0 survivors, final restore re-verified uncached. `go test ./... -count=1` exit 0 across 12 packages; `go build`, `go vet`, `gofmt -l` clean; `tracecheck` exit 0 at 9/394.
.temp/TASK-260830-21gygk-mutants-rev14-02/mutation-source/LOGBOOK.md:900:- FINDING: identical shape to BUG-260902-2faftr (`hasForbiddenConfigName`), one section over and one day later, in code written by someone who had not yet read the entry. It survived the whole positive/negative suite and 17 killed mutants, because every test asserted the gate was *reachable* and none asked which pinned line *declared* it. Nothing about mutation score detects an invented constraint: a mutant that deletes an unlicensed refusal correctly reddens the test that pins the unlicensed refusal.
.temp/TASK-260830-21gygk-mutants-rev14-02/mutation-source/LOGBOOK.md:918:- EVIDENCE: ten mutants, each confirmed present in the source before measuring, all exit 1, no survivors. R1 reverts to the rev2 count wording; R2 flips the sibling row's verdict; R3 deletes the sibling row (delete-only, the coverage requirement fires); R4 lies about the row's own arithmetic, 401→402; R5 drifts the digit 256→512; **R6 narrows production into a genuine count bound** — refuse when `[`+`{` exceed `maxNestingDepth` — which keeps the doc's number true and reddens only because the noun is now driven; R7 adds a false count claim beside the true clause; R8 drifts a rounding row (parser regression control for the new row form); R9 narrows the depth guard to `maxNestingDepth-1`; R10 renames the test the comment cites.
.temp/TASK-260830-21gygk-mutants-rev14-02/mutation-source/LOGBOOK.md:928:- FINDING: the decision survives and is strengthened. `NUM-UNSAFE-ROUND`'s real text describes this implementation more exactly than the row that was quoted, because `validateAXNumbers` refuses on the `json.Number` token before any conversion to a host double. Both fixtures are now cited, each from its own row.
.temp/TASK-260830-21gygk-mutants-rev14-02/mutation-source/LOGBOOK.md:931:- FINDING: presence checks cannot see an added false claim. The first draft required the doc to mention the clause its citations land in, via `strings.Contains`. Mutant N8 — adding "in Section 1.5" beside the true "Section 1.6" — **survived green**. Rewritten as set equality between the clauses the comment names and the clauses the citations land in; N8 then reddens with `names SPEC.md clauses [1.5 1.6], but every citation lands in [1.6]`. A gate that only confirms the truth is present is not a gate against lies being added.
.temp/TASK-260830-21gygk-mutants-rev14-02/mutation-source/LOGBOOK.md:979:- EVIDENCE: review mutant H, an exported `ValidateDecodedRecord(map[string]any)` reaching the guards through that table, left all three pins green and the whole package suite `ok`. Mutant I, the same body as an exported method, survived for the second reason. The producer's own passing mutant D2 called a guard by direct identifier — the single shape the graph could see. Measured coverage was 3/7 guarded functions; `validateSessionLaunchPlan`, `validateSessionBoardIdentity`, `validateSortedUniqueStrings` and `validateProviderIdentityRecord` were pinned vacuously.
.temp/TASK-260830-21gygk-mutants-rev14-02/mutation-source/LOGBOOK.md:982:- DECISION: the published claim was narrowed in the same change. `closed_shapes.go` no longer says the invariant "cannot be broken silently" and `README.md` no longer says a map-taking entry point simply "reddens the pin". Both now state the modelled constructions and name what still evades them: reflection, a function value handed to another package, and a func-typed struct field, since `x.M(...)` resolves only when `M` is a method declared in this package. Fixing the graph without narrowing the claim moves a false guarantee one construction outward rather than removing it.
.temp/TASK-260830-21gygk-mutants-rev14-02/mutation-source/LOGBOOK.md:983:- EVIDENCE: mutants H, I and I2 (byte-parameter method — the receiver is still caller-supplied decoded state) all redden `TestNoExportedEntryPointHandsADecodedValueToAUTF8Recheck`, each naming all 7 guarded functions. Narrowing mutant K removes only the function-value edge: the clean tree stays green and mutant H survives, which is what identifies that edge as the decisive addition rather than something else in the rewrite. Mutant K2 removes the computed-callee edge as well and reproduces the review's measurement exactly — `coverage is 3/7`, naming the same four functions.
.temp/TASK-260830-21gygk-mutants-rev14-02/mutation-source/LOGBOOK.md:994:- EVIDENCE: 14 sibling retargets derived from the document itself — every shared-member pair of the Section 10.4 Git table, both directions — each planted into a copy of the shipped artifact and each required to be refused by the sibling's name. Six mutants run in a throwaway `git archive` copy, all confirmed present before measurement: deleting the anchor reddens all 15 sibling and field plants; narrowing it to `Type` tables only reddens the field-table plant; restoring one of the seven pre-fix citations reddens and names the row and GitHead; removing the table-row boundary reddens both the specdoc and the artifact stitch tests; removing one declaring pin reddens 8 shipped rows rather than granting a free pass; a stale pin and a stale exemption both redden. Class re-scan over all 347 rows: 357 citations, 235 on table body rows, 0 naming another type, 2 naming another member and both exempted by name.
